1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,675
Second block, and this is where structure earns its keep.

2
00:00:03,675 --> 00:00:09,650
Session five ended at a wall: a real relative whose sequence signal had vanished completely.

3
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:12,250
Structure walks through that wall.

4
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:18,775
Session four defined an alignment as a hypothesis about which residues correspond.

5
00:00:18,775 --> 00:00:23,800
That definition does not mention sequences, and that turns out to matter.

6
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:35,775
A sequence alignment decides the correspondence from the sequences alone, using substitution scores — BLOSUM sixty-two and a gap penalty, choosing the arrangement that scores best.

7
00:00:35,775 --> 00:00:44,150
A structure alignment decides the same question from the coordinates, by asking which residues occupy the same position in the two folds.

8
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:52,550
If a residue in one protein sits where a residue in the other protein sits, once the two are optimally superimposed, they correspond.

9
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:54,350
Same question.

10
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:56,900
Completely different evidence.

11
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:05,275
And when the two disagree, the structural answer is usually the one to trust, because it is closer to what we actually mean by correspondence.

12
00:01:05,275 --> 00:01:17,475
Remember also that a sequence alignment can be wrong in a specific, structured way: it can pair up roughly the right regions but put the gaps in the wrong places, so that individual residue pairs are shifted.

13
00:01:17,475 --> 00:01:20,525
We are about to see exactly that happen.

14
00:01:21,125 --> 00:01:27,750
Five proteins compared with beta-globin, both ways, from a close relative to something unrelated.

15
00:01:27,750 --> 00:01:30,425
Watch the two columns diverge.

16
00:01:30,425 --> 00:01:36,300
Human alpha-globin: forty-three per cent identical, TM-score nought point eight eight.

17
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:42,000
Human myoglobin: twenty-five per cent identical, TM-score nought point nine one.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Notice that already — the identity has dropped by eighteen points and the structural similarity has gone *up* slightly.

19
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,325
Human neuroglobin: twenty-two per cent, nought point eight two.

20
00:01:54,325 --> 00:02:01,900
The truncated haemoglobin N from Mycobacterium tuberculosis: sixteen per cent, nought point five seven.

21
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:04,900
Still above the same-fold threshold.

22
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:10,800
And GFP, a genuinely unrelated fold: six per cent, nought point three three.

23
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:13,500
Read the columns against each other.

24
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:24,050
Sequence identity falls from forty-three to sixteen per cent across the four globins, which is the difference between an obvious relative and a sequence you would not look at twice.

25
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:32,175
The TM-score falls from nought point eight eight to nought point five seven, which is the difference between "same fold" and "same fold".

26
00:02:32,175 --> 00:02:37,875
One note on the identity figures: they are measured over the structurally aligned residues.

27
00:02:37,875 --> 00:02:44,600
That is the honest way to do it, because it does not reward an alignment for leaving out the parts it handled badly.

28
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:52,875
Now the column that makes the argument, which is how well the sequence alignment pairs up the same residues the structure alignment does.

29
00:02:52,875 --> 00:02:56,450
Alpha-globin: ninety-five per cent of pairs agree.

30
00:02:56,450 --> 00:02:59,475
Myoglobin: ninety-seven per cent.

31
00:02:59,475 --> 00:03:05,650
For these two the sequence alignment has a strong signal and is essentially getting the right answer.

32
00:03:05,650 --> 00:03:07,900
You do not need structure.

33
00:03:07,900 --> 00:03:10,900
Neuroglobin: sixty-six per cent.

34
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:18,950
The signal is still clear — the score is well above anything shuffled sequences produce — but a third of the residue pairs are wrong.

35
00:03:18,950 --> 00:03:27,925
Not because the alignment missed the relationship, but because the gaps went in the wrong places, shifting stretches of the alignment by a residue or two.

36
00:03:27,925 --> 00:03:31,325
A plausible alignment that is wrong in detail.

37
00:03:31,325 --> 00:03:36,000
Then the truncated haemoglobin, and this is the result to remember.

38
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,225
Its sequence alignment scores twenty-eight.

39
00:03:39,225 --> 00:03:45,175
The best of two hundred alignments against shuffled versions of the same sequence scores forty-nine.

40
00:03:45,175 --> 00:03:48,350
The real relative scores *worse than noise*.

41
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:52,175
And the fraction of residue pairs it gets right is zero.

42
00:03:52,175 --> 00:03:55,600
There is no parameter you can tune to fix that.

43
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:58,250
It is not a threshold problem.

44
00:03:58,250 --> 00:04:01,125
The pairwise sequence signal is gone.

45
00:04:01,125 --> 00:04:10,625
And yet the structure alignment finds the globin fold over more than a hundred residues, and CATH puts both proteins in the same homologous superfamily.

46
00:04:10,625 --> 00:04:16,025
That is the central lesson of this session: structure is more conserved than sequence.

47
00:04:16,025 --> 00:04:28,150
When the sequence evidence has faded to nothing, structural evidence can still show the relationship — because what evolution is conserving is the fold, and the sequence is only its implementation.

48
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:36,150
And recall from Session five that BLAST with default settings missed myoglobin — which is two rows above the hard case here.

49
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:39,325
Heuristic search makes the gap wider still.

50
00:04:39,925 --> 00:04:44,700
Two numbers for structural similarity, and they behave quite differently.

51
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:55,325
RMSD, root-mean-square deviation, is the most direct: the root-mean-square distance between corresponding atoms once the two structures are optimally superimposed.

52
00:04:55,325 --> 00:04:58,125
Simple, and it has three problems.

53
00:04:58,125 --> 00:05:04,225
It grows with protein length, so RMSDs for proteins of different sizes are not comparable.

54
00:05:04,225 --> 00:05:12,650
One badly placed loop inflates it even when everything else matches perfectly, because squaring the distances gives the outliers all the weight.

55
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:21,875
And it depends on which atoms you compare: restrict the superposition to the residues that fit well and you can report a flattering number about a poor model.

56
00:05:21,875 --> 00:05:30,900
So an RMSD should always be quoted with the number of aligned residues: "one point five ångström over a hundred and forty residues".

57
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:34,150
An RMSD on its own is not a claim.

58
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:36,950
The TM-score fixes all three.

59
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:48,550
It weights each residue pair by how close it is — small deviations count fully, large ones hardly at all, so a single bad loop cannot dominate — and it normalises by protein length.

60
00:05:48,550 --> 00:06:01,900
The distance scale is d-zero equals one point two four times the cube root of L minus fifteen, minus one point eight; for beta-globin's hundred and forty-six residues that is about four and a half ångström.

61
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:15,850
The notebook's RMSDs for the same five proteins: alpha-globin one point four ångström, myoglobin one point five, neuroglobin two point nought, truncated haemoglobin three point two, GFP five point one.

62
00:06:15,850 --> 00:06:29,700
Compare those with the TM-scores — myoglobin at one point five ångström and nought point nine one is unambiguous, while the truncated haemoglobin at three point two ångström sounds bad and is still the same fold.

63
00:06:29,700 --> 00:06:42,300
One dependency both share: before either can be computed, one structure has to be moved onto the other by a rigid-body transformation — translate the centre of mass, rotate about it, change nothing inside.

64
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:50,775
Programs report the three-by-three rotation matrix and the translation vector, and they choose the superposition that minimises RMSD.

65
00:06:50,775 --> 00:06:56,075
So a published RMSD is always the best RMSD, never an arbitrary one.

66
00:06:56,075 --> 00:06:59,600
And some measures avoid superposition entirely.

67
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:08,350
The global distance test, GDT, asks what fraction of distances match the reference within a cutoff, averaged over several cutoffs.

68
00:07:08,350 --> 00:07:13,950
That is the measure CASP has used to rank structure predictions for twenty-five years.

69
00:07:14,550 --> 00:07:17,600
How to read a TM-score in practice.

70
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:21,350
It runs from zero to one, where one is identical.

71
00:07:21,350 --> 00:07:26,600
Unrelated proteins typically land between nought point two and nought point three.

72
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:31,275
Above about nought point five usually means the same overall fold.

73
00:07:31,275 --> 00:07:37,175
That is a rule of thumb, not a law, and it is worth being honest about where it is reliable.

74
00:07:37,175 --> 00:07:39,900
Our examples at the ends are clear.

75
00:07:39,900 --> 00:07:46,950
The truncated haemoglobin at nought point five seven is genuinely the same fold, and CATH agrees.

76
00:07:46,950 --> 00:07:50,850
GFP at nought point three three is genuinely not.

77
00:07:50,850 --> 00:07:59,575
But a score of nought point four eight is not evidence that two proteins have different folds, and nought point five two is not proof that they share one.

78
00:07:59,575 --> 00:08:08,025
Scores in the middle need other evidence — a classification, a domain assignment, conserved functional residues, anything independent.

79
00:08:08,025 --> 00:08:14,575
A number on a continuous scale with a threshold drawn across it will always be ambiguous near the threshold.

80
00:08:14,575 --> 00:08:20,350
That is not a defect in the TM-score; it is what fold similarity is actually like.

81
00:08:20,950 --> 00:08:27,550
DALI, from Holm and Sander in nineteen ninety-three, uses the distance-map idea from the first block.

82
00:08:27,550 --> 00:08:40,275
The reasoning: if two proteins have the same fold, their distance maps should look alike, even when their sequences do not — because the map records internal geometry, and the fold is the internal geometry.

83
00:08:40,275 --> 00:08:49,550
So rather than superimposing coordinates, DALI looks for residue correspondences that make the two patterns of intramolecular distances agree.

84
00:08:49,550 --> 00:08:51,750
The figure makes the case.

85
00:08:51,750 --> 00:08:59,875
Beta-globin and myoglobin, only a quarter of their residues identical, give distance maps that are almost indistinguishable.

86
00:08:59,875 --> 00:09:07,275
GFP's beta-barrel gives a completely different pattern — not a degraded version of the globin pattern, a different one.

87
00:09:07,275 --> 00:09:11,400
The analogy with Session four is direct and worth stating.

88
00:09:11,400 --> 00:09:15,825
A sequence alignment compares residues along two sequences.

89
00:09:15,825 --> 00:09:20,100
DALI compares patterns of residue-to-residue geometry.

90
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:23,675
Same alignment idea, different thing being aligned.

91
00:09:23,675 --> 00:09:35,150
DALI and TM-align — Zhang and Skolnick, two thousand and five, which finds the superposition maximising the TM-score — are accurate and still used for careful pairwise comparisons.

92
00:09:35,150 --> 00:09:41,950
And they are far too slow to search hundreds of millions of structures, which is the problem the next slides solve.

93
00:09:42,550 --> 00:09:46,450
Put the two halves of the course's search story side by side.

94
00:09:46,450 --> 00:09:52,850
For sequences, Session five: BLAST and MMseqs2, searching with the amino-acid sequence.

95
00:09:52,850 --> 00:09:57,500
For structures, today: Foldseek, searching with a structural sequence.

96
00:09:57,500 --> 00:10:01,500
That phrase is doing the work, and the next slide unpacks it.

97
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:10,325
Foldseek comes from the same group as MMseqs2, and it is tens of thousands of times faster than TM-align at comparable sensitivity.

98
00:10:10,325 --> 00:10:14,300
Notice the shape of the problem is the same as Session five's.

99
00:10:14,300 --> 00:10:21,450
An accurate pairwise method exists — TM-align there, Smith-Waterman here — and it is too slow for a database.

100
00:10:21,450 --> 00:10:27,800
The answer in both cases is to change what you are comparing so that fast machinery applies.

101
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:32,725
Here is the idea, and it is one of the most elegant in the course.

102
00:10:32,725 --> 00:10:44,650
For each residue, Foldseek looks at the local three-dimensional environment — the geometry between that residue and its nearest neighbour in space — and assigns one of twenty discrete structural states.

103
00:10:44,650 --> 00:10:47,350
That is the 3Di alphabet.

104
00:10:47,350 --> 00:10:51,900
A structure becomes a string of 3Di letters, one per residue.

105
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:56,800
And once a structure is a string, you can search it with sequence machinery.

106
00:10:56,800 --> 00:11:06,200
The same prefilter-and-align cascade as MMseqs2: index the database by k-mers, find double hits on a diagonal, filter, then align.

107
00:11:06,200 --> 00:11:10,200
No geometric superposition anywhere in the fast path.

108
00:11:10,200 --> 00:11:20,250
So: coordinates, to local geometry around each residue, to one 3Di state per residue, to a structural sequence, to fast alignment and database search.

109
00:11:20,250 --> 00:11:27,825
The hundreds of millions of structures in the PDB and the AlphaFold Database become searchable in seconds to minutes.

110
00:11:27,825 --> 00:11:31,450
Now the part that belongs to the second half of this course.

111
00:11:31,450 --> 00:11:34,850
Nobody designed those twenty states by hand.

112
00:11:34,850 --> 00:11:48,075
A machine-learning model — a variational autoencoder — was trained on pairs of structurally aligned residues to find a compact set of recurring three-dimensional environments that are conserved between related structures.

113
00:11:48,075 --> 00:11:50,700
Compare that with Session five.

114
00:11:50,700 --> 00:12:00,125
There, humans chose the representation, amino acids, and humans chose the scoring scheme, BLOSUM sixty-two, by counting substitutions.

115
00:12:00,125 --> 00:12:04,125
In Foldseek, machine learning chose the representation.

116
00:12:04,125 --> 00:12:12,425
You do not need to know how a variational autoencoder works to take the point: a useful representation can itself be learned from data.

117
00:12:12,425 --> 00:12:15,800
Sessions seven to twelve build that machinery.

118
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:26,250
A real run, on three structures: the two haemoglobin states we use throughout — two-H-H-B, the deoxy form, and one-H-H-O, the oxy form — and GFP.

119
00:12:26,250 --> 00:12:37,250
Matching alpha to alpha, or beta to beta, between the two haemoglobin states gives a TM-score of nought point nine eight and an RMSD of nought point seven ångström.

120
00:12:37,250 --> 00:12:46,175
These are the same protein in two different conformational states, and that is about as similar as two independently solved structures ever get.

121
00:12:46,175 --> 00:12:54,725
Which tells you something important for the third block: whatever the difference between the T and R states is, it is not in the individual chains.

122
00:12:54,725 --> 00:13:07,425
Alpha-globin against beta-globin, forty-three per cent identical, gives a TM-score of nought point nine zero to nought point nine one, with RMSDs of one point seven to one point eight ångström.

123
00:13:07,425 --> 00:13:13,675
Different genes, separated by an ancient duplication — and structurally almost interchangeable.

124
00:13:13,675 --> 00:13:21,100
And searching two-H-H-B against GFP returns no hits at all, even with a loosened significance threshold.

125
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:23,425
Not a weak hit: nothing.

126
00:13:23,425 --> 00:13:30,725
Which is the right answer, and worth seeing, because a search tool that always returns something is not telling you anything.

